WEBVTT

00:00.330 --> 00:02.400
-: Alright, so next up I wanna talk

00:02.400 --> 00:03.690
about a different command

00:03.690 --> 00:05.070
you may come across

00:05.070 --> 00:06.810
that we can use to switch branches.

00:06.810 --> 00:08.730
It's called git checkout.

00:08.730 --> 00:09.630
You can see it here

00:09.630 --> 00:11.790
git checkout, with a branch name.

00:11.790 --> 00:13.650
We'll do the exact same thing

00:13.650 --> 00:16.530
as git switch with the same branch name.

00:16.530 --> 00:20.382
It just switches us to whatever this branch name is.

00:20.382 --> 00:23.820
So this command git checkout. existed for a long time.

00:23.820 --> 00:25.260
It still exists in Gits,

00:25.260 --> 00:29.910
and it does a lot more than just switching branches.

00:29.910 --> 00:32.730
So it's, it's kind of a Swiss Army knife of Git.

00:32.730 --> 00:34.020
It doesn't do everything.

00:34.020 --> 00:35.760
I guess neither does a Swiss Army knife

00:35.760 --> 00:37.710
but it does a lot more.

00:37.710 --> 00:41.100
And for a lot of users this was confusing

00:41.100 --> 00:44.040
and just it felt like it did too many things.

00:44.040 --> 00:46.230
So git switch was introduced.

00:46.230 --> 00:47.100
It's newer.

00:47.100 --> 00:48.240
So the command we've been using,

00:48.240 --> 00:49.440
git switch is the newer

00:49.440 --> 00:51.660
fancier way of switching branches.

00:51.660 --> 00:54.030
But this is the old school way and it still works.

00:54.030 --> 00:56.370
And I'm showing it to you because you'll still see this

00:56.370 --> 00:59.340
for sure all over the place in documentation

00:59.340 --> 01:02.460
or on blog posts, YouTube videos, that sort of thing.

01:02.460 --> 01:05.880
Co-workers might use git checkout to switch branches

01:05.880 --> 01:09.300
plus git switch is still liable to change

01:09.300 --> 01:11.430
although it's been around now for a little while,

01:11.430 --> 01:13.470
I don't expect it to.

01:13.470 --> 01:15.270
So lemme just demonstrate this.

01:15.270 --> 01:17.220
I'm on empty playlist

01:17.220 --> 01:19.470
you can see I've got my four branches.

01:19.470 --> 01:21.420
Let's switch to oldies.

01:21.420 --> 01:22.740
So instead of git switch

01:22.740 --> 01:25.173
I'll do git checkout oldies,

01:26.640 --> 01:28.050
and it's the same behavior.

01:28.050 --> 01:30.270
I'm now on the oldies branch,

01:30.270 --> 01:31.440
as we can see there

01:31.440 --> 01:33.720
and my file has been updated to reflect what's

01:33.720 --> 01:35.340
on the oldies branch.

01:35.340 --> 01:36.780
So that's kind of all there is

01:36.780 --> 01:39.870
to using git checkout in that one particular way.

01:39.870 --> 01:42.270
But remember, git checkout does a lot more.

01:42.270 --> 01:43.560
You can read about it on the docs,

01:43.560 --> 01:46.500
although I really recommend you don't read it right now.

01:46.500 --> 01:48.060
Just know this first part.

01:48.060 --> 01:49.980
We can use it to switch branches.

01:49.980 --> 01:51.330
But then there's this whole other thing,

01:51.330 --> 01:53.370
restore working tree files.

01:53.370 --> 01:55.830
Look at all the different options for how we use it.

01:55.830 --> 01:58.350
We will get into checkout later on,

01:58.350 --> 02:00.030
but in terms of switching branches,

02:00.030 --> 02:01.530
it's very, very simple.

02:01.530 --> 02:04.200
With that said, I still prefer git switch.

02:04.200 --> 02:06.090
Now another thing that we can do

02:06.090 --> 02:08.040
is instead of creating a branch

02:08.040 --> 02:11.580
and then switching to it in two separate steps,

02:11.580 --> 02:13.470
we can do it all in one go.

02:13.470 --> 02:15.330
We can do it with git switch

02:15.330 --> 02:19.740
if we add dash C as an option for "create".

02:19.740 --> 02:23.730
So we provide a branch name and it will create that branch

02:23.730 --> 02:26.400
and then switch us over to it, all at once.

02:26.400 --> 02:28.290
So this can be pretty useful in my opinion.

02:28.290 --> 02:30.990
Let's make a new branch from,

02:30.990 --> 02:32.940
let's go back to master maybe.

02:32.940 --> 02:34.770
So we'll go back to master.

02:34.770 --> 02:37.740
We'll use switch, or check out, it doesn't matter.

02:37.740 --> 02:39.930
Alright, so I'm gonna make my new branch

02:39.930 --> 02:41.490
with this shortcut I showed you

02:41.490 --> 02:44.940
git switch, dash C

02:44.940 --> 02:47.130
and then a branch name that doesn't exist yet.

02:47.130 --> 02:49.110
So I'll switch, I'm gonna call it

02:49.110 --> 02:52.600
recentish music

02:53.670 --> 02:58.020
like that, and notice it made it and I switched to it.

02:58.020 --> 02:59.520
So without the dash C,

02:59.520 --> 03:01.350
it would give me an error, right?

03:01.350 --> 03:03.030
Just like this error,

03:03.030 --> 03:04.634
it can't find that branch to switch to.

03:04.634 --> 03:07.530
But with dash C, it makes the branch for me.

03:07.530 --> 03:09.510
And then switches over

03:09.510 --> 03:12.810
and I'll just add two songs. for some recent music.

03:12.810 --> 03:15.060
I'll remove ABBA, Phoebe Bridgers.

03:15.060 --> 03:16.470
By the way, if you don't know her,

03:16.470 --> 03:18.780
very talented singer-songwriter.

03:18.780 --> 03:21.033
And I'll just add and commit that.

03:22.350 --> 03:24.060
I'll do it in two steps this time.

03:24.060 --> 03:26.130
Okay, add two Phoebe Bridger songs.

03:26.130 --> 03:27.107
There we go.

03:27.107 --> 03:29.670
And that's kind of all I wanted to show you here.

03:29.670 --> 03:33.210
Now there is an equivalent command if you use git checkout

03:33.210 --> 03:36.900
but unfortunately it's get checkout dash B

03:36.900 --> 03:39.150
B as in boy, B for branch.

03:39.150 --> 03:40.080
It's very confusing.

03:40.080 --> 03:41.634
I don't know why they went with dash B

03:41.634 --> 03:42.467
(laughs)

03:42.467 --> 03:44.310
but it's just how it works.

03:44.310 --> 03:47.010
If you wanna make a branch and check it out in one go

03:47.010 --> 03:50.370
git checkout dash B or git switch dash C.

03:50.370 --> 03:54.330
And again, I prefer this, but you'll see git checkout used.

03:54.330 --> 03:56.580
All right, so we've got some pretty good practice now

03:56.580 --> 03:59.130
making branches and switching, making new commits

03:59.130 --> 04:01.290
on separate branches, switching between them.

04:01.290 --> 04:02.970
Hopefully it's clear

04:02.970 --> 04:05.580
or at least becoming clear how this all works.

04:05.580 --> 04:06.750
One thing though,

04:06.750 --> 04:10.740
that my limited example has not illustrated is

04:10.740 --> 04:13.440
that we can have as many files as needed, right?

04:13.440 --> 04:14.400
I don't want you to think

04:14.400 --> 04:18.000
that branches exist on a per file basis.

04:18.000 --> 04:20.340
We do see this one file being updated

04:20.340 --> 04:21.900
but I could make another file

04:21.900 --> 04:23.520
on one of these branches and

04:23.520 --> 04:25.920
Git will make sure that it exists on that branch

04:25.920 --> 04:27.660
and does not exist on the other branches.

04:27.660 --> 04:31.350
Or Git will update all relevant files and folders

04:31.350 --> 04:32.460
depending on the branch.

04:32.460 --> 04:34.740
So it's not limited to just one file

04:34.740 --> 04:36.990
even though my simple demo has been.

04:36.990 --> 04:38.940
All right? So that's it for this video.
